1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,188
Lesson 11.5.

2
00:00:02,568 --> 00:00:05,131
LLM research agents.

3
00:00:06,250 --> 00:00:08,918
In the previous lesson
we graded proposals.

4
00:00:09,297 --> 00:00:10,989
Now we train the proposer.

5
00:00:11,369 --> 00:00:14,988
If a language model receives
the reward of each attempt, it

6
00:00:14,988 --> 00:00:18,361
can learn by reinforcement
to write better experiments.

7
00:00:18,741 --> 00:00:23,491
Extropic, with Prime Intellect, tried
this in October twenty twenty-six, and

8
00:00:23,491 --> 00:00:27,679
called it the first sparks of
thermodynamic recursive intelligence.

9
00:00:28,059 --> 00:00:30,778
Let's look at the method,
our own experiment on

10
00:00:30,778 --> 00:00:33,265
cheating, and what was
really demonstrated.

11
00:00:34,383 --> 00:00:37,784
GRPO, group relative
policy optimization, was

12
00:00:37,784 --> 00:00:41,637
introduced with DeepSeekMath
in twenty twenty-four.

13
00:00:42,017 --> 00:00:45,178
For each task, the model
generates a group of attempts.

14
00:00:45,558 --> 00:00:48,784
The advantage of each attempt
is its reward minus the

15
00:00:48,784 --> 00:00:52,071
group mean, divided by the
group's standard deviation.

16
00:00:52,451 --> 00:00:56,956
Attempts better than average are
reinforced and worse ones penalized, with

17
00:00:56,956 --> 00:01:00,790
no need for a value network that
estimates the expected reward.

18
00:01:01,917 --> 00:01:04,672
Extropic's reward combines two signals.

19
00:01:05,052 --> 00:01:09,047
Execution, with seventy percent of
the weight, is deterministic: the

20
00:01:09,047 --> 00:01:12,690
code runs and reproduces the
reference metric, or it does not.

21
00:01:13,070 --> 00:01:16,909
The rubric, with thirty percent,
is graded by a judge model,

22
00:01:16,909 --> 00:01:20,748
Nemotron 3 Super with one hundred
twenty billion parameters,

23
00:01:20,748 --> 00:01:23,756
with criteria written in
advance for each task.

24
00:01:24,136 --> 00:01:28,169
The authors give more weight to
execution because it is harder to fool.

25
00:01:29,283 --> 00:01:31,765
We test it with a toy GRPO.

26
00:01:32,145 --> 00:01:36,271
The policy chooses among the five
proposals of the previous lesson, with

27
00:01:36,271 --> 00:01:40,454
groups of eight and three hundred
steps, averaged over two hundred seeds.

28
00:01:40,834 --> 00:01:44,538
With the full reward, the
policy ends up choosing

29
00:01:44,538 --> 00:01:47,863
the correct method 99.8
percent of the time.

30
00:01:48,243 --> 00:01:53,066
With execution only, the correct
method drops to thirty-two percent: the

31
00:01:53,066 --> 00:01:57,285
incorrect sampler keeps thirty-three
and the cheat thirty-five.

32
00:01:57,665 --> 00:02:00,890
The agent learns exactly
what the reward rewards.

33
00:02:02,017 --> 00:02:07,755
The real experiment trained Qwen 3.6,
with thirty-five billion parameters and

34
00:02:07,755 --> 00:02:13,271
three billion active, for one hundred
GRPO steps, about twenty-five hours,

35
00:02:13,651 --> 00:02:17,470
on about fifty tasks that
reproduce classic experiments such

36
00:02:17,470 --> 00:02:20,717
as Boltzmann machines and
the wake-sleep algorithm.

37
00:02:21,097 --> 00:02:28,371
The reward on unseen tasks rose
from 0.127 to 0.361, almost triple.

38
00:02:28,751 --> 00:02:39,559
For reference, on the same set, Claude
Opus 4.8 scored 0.464 and GPT 5.5, 0.747.

39
00:02:40,683 --> 00:02:41,937
Let's read carefully.

40
00:02:42,317 --> 00:02:44,710
What was demonstrated
is that a small model

41
00:02:44,710 --> 00:02:47,381
improves a lot at reproducing
known experiments.

42
00:02:47,761 --> 00:02:51,274
What the name promises, agents
that discover new algorithms

43
00:02:51,274 --> 00:02:54,430
for the hardware and improve
it in a recursive cycle,

44
00:02:54,810 --> 00:02:59,568
is for now a goal: the authors say the
next step is to move from reproducing

45
00:02:59,568 --> 00:03:03,888
to discovering, and that the tasks
do not yet run on their simulator.

46
00:03:04,268 --> 00:03:06,463
Nor do they report
observations of cheating.

47
00:03:06,843 --> 00:03:09,260
They are first sparks, as the title says.

48
00:03:10,383 --> 00:03:16,032
In practice, GRPO over verifiable tasks,
a reward dominated by execution with

49
00:03:16,032 --> 00:03:20,947
a judge for the rubric, and auditing
of runs in search of cheating.

50
00:03:21,327 --> 00:03:25,251
In lab thirty you will evaluate
language models on your tasks.

51
00:03:26,383 --> 00:03:29,141
Common mistakes:
rewarding only the metric.

52
00:03:29,521 --> 00:03:32,339
Trusting a judge without
criteria fixed in advance.

53
00:03:32,719 --> 00:03:35,777
Reading recursive self-improvement
as something demonstrated.

54
00:03:36,157 --> 00:03:39,549
And comparing scores
over different task sets.

55
00:03:40,650 --> 00:03:45,445
In summary, GRPO reinforces attempts
better than their group's average.

56
00:03:45,825 --> 00:03:50,063
The reward mixes execution and
rubric, and our experiment shows that

57
00:03:50,063 --> 00:03:53,989
without a rubric the agent learns
cheats and incorrect methods.

58
00:03:54,369 --> 00:03:59,064
Thermo-RSI almost tripled the reward
of a small model at reproducing

59
00:03:59,064 --> 00:04:02,999
experiments; discovering new
algorithms remains the goal.

60
00:04:04,117 --> 00:04:07,226
To practice: compute the
advantages of a group.

61
00:04:07,606 --> 00:04:10,927
Explain why GRPO does
not need a value network.

62
00:04:11,307 --> 00:04:14,581
Explain why the policy without
a rubric chose the cheat.

63
00:04:14,961 --> 00:04:18,152
And say what would really demonstrate
recursive self-improvement.
